<h3>Exploration · analysis</h3>
<ul>
    <li><strong>explore</strong> — skims the codebase and finds the files and structures relevant to the task.</li>
    <li><strong>analyst</strong> — analyzes code or requirements and lays out the essentials and the points at issue.</li>
    <li><strong>architect</strong> — sets the overall structure and design direction, and verifies that the result matches the design.</li>
    <li><strong>planner</strong> — breaks the work into executable steps and builds a plan.</li>
    <li><strong>critic</strong> — critically reviews a plan or a result to find missing parts and risks.</li>
    <li><strong>tracer</strong> — traces the cause of a bug or failure by following the code flow.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Implementation · fixing</h3>
<ul>
    <li><strong>executor</strong> — writes and modifies the actual code according to the plan. It is divided into lightweight, standard, and high-performance tiers depending on the difficulty of the task.</li>
    <li><strong>debugger</strong> — finds the cause of a bug and fixes it.</li>
    <li><strong>code-simplifier</strong> — tidies up tangled, complicated code into something simpler and easier to read (refactoring).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Verification · quality</h3>
<ul>
    <li><strong>qa-tester</strong> — tests whether a feature works as intended and gathers evidence of completion.</li>
    <li><strong>test-engineer</strong> — writes and configures automated test code.</li>
    <li><strong>code-reviewer</strong> — reviews code quality and style.</li>
    <li><strong>security-reviewer</strong> — checks for security vulnerabilities.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Documentation · design · data</h3>
<ul>
    <li><strong>writer</strong> — writes documents and explanatory text.</li>
    <li><strong>document-specialist</strong> — finds, checks, and organizes external library and API documentation.</li>
    <li><strong>designer</strong> — handles screen UI/UX design.</li>
    <li><strong>vision</strong> — analyzes images such as screen captures or diagrams.</li>
    <li><strong>scientist</strong> — analyzes data.</li>
    <li><strong>git-master</strong> — handles Git work such as branches, commits, and merges.</li>
</ul>

<div class="note">
    <span class="nh">In one line</span>
    You do not need to pick agents yourself. Modes like autopilot grasp the intent of the work and place the needed agents for you, so normally you only have to say "what to do".
</div>

<h3>skillify — turning repetitive work into a skill</h3>
<p><code>/oh-my-claudecode:skillify</code> is the command that extracts work patterns that were effective in one session into reusable skills. Organize a flow you repeat often just once, and you can call it up right away in your next task. The former name <code>learner</code> is a deprecated (support to be discontinued) compatibility alias, so use skillify in new documents and training.</p>
<pre><code><span class="c"># Extract patterns that were effective in the session into reusable skills</span>
/oh-my-claudecode:skillify</code></pre>

<h3>Hook · Memory</h3>
<p>OMC makes use of Claude Code's Hook events to handle keyword detection, skill injection, state recovery, and completion verification. As of the latest Reference, the Hook events are broken down more finely.</p>

<h4>Things to know</h4>
<ul>
    <li><strong>autopilot · ralph · ultrawork · ultraqa are skills, not Hooks</strong>. keyword-detector activates them, and the persistent-mode Hook assists with keeping them running.</li>
    <li>To turn off all Hooks use <code>DISABLE_OMC</code>; to exclude only specific Hooks use <code>OMC_SKIP_HOOKS</code>.</li>
    <li>Windows Native Hooks were improved after v4.14.4 and can be used as they are. Some features that require tmux also work without WSL if you use <strong>psmux</strong> (a tmux-compatible tool for native Windows), per the current official README, and WSL2 is the alternative path.</li>
</ul>
<pre><code><span class="c"># Disable all of OMC's Hooks at once</span>
export DISABLE_OMC=1
<span class="c"># Exclude only the specified Hooks (the rest work normally)</span>
export OMC_SKIP_HOOKS=PreToolUse,PostToolUse</code></pre>

<h4>When Hooks run</h4>
<p>A Hook is an action that automatically steps in and runs at each specific event in Claude Code. OMC uses this to inject context and turn skills on automatically. There are four main timings.</p>

